-- title:
--   Eq. (4): Jensen step $R(q)\le R_s(\tilde q)$
-- statement:
--   Let $q$ be a dynamic pricing policy and $\pi$ a probability distribution on the states. Define the averaged fractions $\tilde q_{ij}=\sum_{x\in S}\pi(x)q_{ij}(x)$. Then
--
--   $$
--   \sum_{x\in S}\pi(x)\sum_{(i,j)\in\mathcal A}\Lambda_{ij}q_{ij}(x)I_{ij}(q_{ij}(x))\ \le\ \sum_{(i,j)\in\mathcal A}\Lambda_{ij}\tilde q_{ij}I_{ij}(\tilde q_{ij})=R_s(\tilde q).
--   $$
--
--   This is display (4) in the proof of Proposition 1: it follows from the concavity of $q\mapsto qI_{ij}(q)$ and Jensen's inequality. It bounds the reward of any dynamic policy by the fluid reward of its averaged fractions.
--
--   Differences from the source:
--   - reading: trip durations i.i.d. with a general distribution with rational Laplace transform and mean 1/μij, represented by a network of exponential stages (p. 4) → a phase-type law per pair: finitely many stages, entry probabilities β (total ≤ 1; the defect is a zero-length trip), rates r between stages, exit rates e, an exit reachable from every stage; 1/μij = β(−T)⁻¹𝟙; reason: the paper's state counts vehicles at each stage of the transit queue (footnote 1), so the model exists exactly for laws with such a stage network, i.e. phase-type laws; the general (not only Coxian) form is taken
--   - encoding: demand, prices, rewards and travel data on the pairs (i,j) ∈ 𝒜 (Λij > 0) → all of them given on every pair of locations; reason: pairs with Λij = 0 enter every rate, sum and constraint with factor 0; no instance changes
--   - encoding: prices pij with acceptance fractions qij = 1 − Fij(pij) (p. 5) → the fractions q ∈ [0,1] and the per-trip rewards Iij(q) as primitives, q·Iij(q) concave on [0,1]; reason: the paper reformulates the policy in terms of the quantiles q (p. 5); concavity of q·I is its only requirement
--   - generalization: the optimal policy q* and its steady-state probability π* → any dynamic policy q and any probability vector π on the states; reason: Jensen's inequality uses only π ≥ 0 and Σπ = 1; q* and π* are a special case
--   - generalization: the network generated by 𝒩 and 𝒜 is strongly connected (p. 4, without loss of generality) → no connectivity assumption on the demand graph; reason: no step uses it; strongly connected instances are a special case
-- source:
--   Benjaafar, Shen, Pricing in On-Demand (and One-Way) Vehicle Sharing Networks, SSRN 3998297 (version of 2023-01-10), https://ssrn.com/abstract=3998297, proof of Proposition 1, eq. (4), pp. 6–7

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_VehShare_SeqBounds_reward
import Definitions.Def_VehShare_SeqBounds_Rs

namespace VehShare.SeqBounds

/-- **Jensen step (4).**
Benjaafar, Shen, *Pricing in On-Demand (and One-Way) Vehicle Sharing Networks*, SSRN 3998297
(version of 2023-01-10), proof of Proposition 1, display (4), p. 6.

For every dynamic policy `q` and every probability vector `π` on the states,
`R(q, π) ≤ R_s(q̃)` with `q̃_ij = ∑_x π(x) q_ij(x)`, because `q ↦ q I_ij(q)` is concave on `[0, 1]`.

**Formalization Note.** Stationarity of `π` is not needed (a generalization); `π` is any probability
vector. -/
theorem jensen_reward_le (P : Instance) (K : ℕ) (q : Policy P K) (π : State P K → ℝ)
    (hπ0 : ∀ z, 0 ≤ π z) (hπ1 : ∑ z, π z = 1) :
    reward q π ≤ Rs P (fun i j => ∑ z, π z * q.1 z i j) := by sorry

end VehShare.SeqBounds
